<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Main results (RQ1)</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The experimental results are summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Setup and baselines ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Mint: A Simple Test-Time Adaptation of Vision-Language Models against Common Corruptions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. We observe that training-free methods generally perform worse, as they do not update the model during adaptation. Among them, TPS achieves relatively strong performance by adjusting the text embeddings. CLIPArTT and WATT-S, which allow updates to the image encoder, perform best among the baselines. However, these methods do not share information across batches, which limits their overall effectiveness. Across all settings, <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mint</span> consistently improves accuracy and achieves the best performance. Compared to the strongest baselines, <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mint</span> yields absolute gains of 3.9%, 2.2%, and 3.1%, respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Accuracy (mean <math id="S5.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> s.d. %) of <span id="S5.T2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mint</span> with various batch size.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:33.3pt;vertical-align:-15.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-146.0pt,14.1pt) scale(0.541553128130678,0.541553128130678) ;">
<table id="S5.T2.4.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
